<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Working offline – Climbing Logbook</title>
  <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="../../logbook/favicon-32.png">
  <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="16x16" href="../../logbook/favicon-16.png">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#ff2727">
  <!-- #470 -- starts the brand font fetch during initial parsing rather
       than waiting for layout to discover climbing-header.js's own
       @font-face rule -- see public/index.html's own comment for the
       fuller reasoning. Absolute /logbook/... here too, not ../../logbook/...
       like the rest of this page's own asset paths -- the preload only
       helps if its URL matches climbing-header.js's own hardcoded
       @font-face url() byte-for-byte. -->
  <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" href="/logbook/fonts/BebasNeue-Regular.woff2" crossorigin>
  <!-- Generated by `pnpm run tailwind:build` (see styles/tailwind.css) --
       not committed, see .gitignore. Same stylesheet the main app uses. -->
  <!-- #857 -- ?v={{ assetVersion }} (see .eleventy.js's own comment)
       lets public/_headers cache these two files immutably; skipped in
       dev (isDevBuild), which doesn't need it (see app-layout.njk's own
       fuller comment on why). -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="../../logbook/tailwind.css{% if not isDevBuild %}?v={{ assetVersion }}{% endif %}">
  <!-- Shared design tokens + <climbing-header> Custom Element (#345).
       Classic script, not a module -- must run synchronously here, before
       first paint. See the file's own comment for why. -->
  <script src="../../logbook/components/climbing-header.js{% if not isDevBuild %}?v={{ assetVersion }}{% endif %}"></script>
  <script>
    (function () {
      var stored = localStorage.getItem("logbook_theme");
      var theme = stored || (matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: light)").matches ? "light" : "dark");
      document.documentElement.dataset.theme = theme;
    })();
  </script>
</head>
<!-- #847 -- unlike login/register/reset-password/apex, this page does
     NOT redirect a logged-in visitor away: help content is meant to be
     reachable from inside the app (the burger menu's own Help link)
     just as much as from a logged-out visitor, so there's no session-
     redirect.js wiring here and nothing is hidden pending a session
     check -- see #190's own body for that distinction. -->
<body class="bg-background text-foreground font-sans min-h-screen flex flex-col items-center px-4 pt-10 pb-16">
  <div class="w-full max-w-[560px]">
    <climbing-header variant="brand"></climbing-header>
    <div class="bg-surface border border-border rounded-app p-6 mt-2">
      <h1 class="text-[1.3rem] font-bold text-foreground mb-3">Working offline</h1>
      <!-- #847 -- placeholder only: the real content (what actually
           happens while offline, how sync resolves once you're back,
           what "unsynced" looks like) is tracked on #190 (the help
           section's own foundational content pass), not written here --
           this page exists so the burger menu's new status row (#847)
           has a real, non-404 Help link to point at in the meantime. -->
      <p class="text-muted mb-3">
        This page is coming soon. It'll explain how Climbing Logbook works
        without a connection — what's saved locally, when it syncs, and
        what the sync status in the menu is telling you.
      </p>
      <p class="text-muted">
        In short: everything you log is saved on your device first, and
        synced automatically whenever you're back online. Nothing is
        lost by being offline at the crag.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="text-center mt-6">
      <a class="text-accent" href="/">Back to Climbing Logbook</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
